WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.300
Why Finpoint?

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.690
Your bank keeps a record of every payment you make.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.055
What it can’t tell you is what each payment was really for.

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:11.493
Say you spent $80 at the supermarket.

00:00:11.863 --> 00:00:16.933
$50 of that was groceries, and $30 was a birthday gift for a friend.

00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:19.783
Your bank shows one $80 payment.

00:00:20.073 --> 00:00:22.991
But to you, those are two very different things.

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:29.707
Or you paid $90 for dinner with a friend, and they sent their $60 share back a week later.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:33.047
Your bank shows two payments, a week apart:

00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:34.887
money out, and money in.

00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:37.839
What dinner really cost you was $30.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.530
Finpoint closes that gap.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:46.530
It keeps your bank activity exactly as your bank reports it, and lets you explain it in your own words:

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.690
groceries, $50.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.660
A gift, $30.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.034
Your share of dinner, $30.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:59.640
Everything else in Finpoint, from spending charts to budgets, is built on those explanations.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.870
So using Finpoint comes down to three things:

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.461
bring in your bank activity, explain it, and see where your money really went.

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:11.381
Statements, Records and Allocations

00:01:11.981 --> 00:01:15.641
Finpoint uses three words for this, and they’re worth getting to know:

00:01:15.911 --> 00:01:18.378
Statements, Records and Allocations.

00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:24.203
A Statement is one entry of bank activity, exactly as your bank reports it.

00:01:24.493 --> 00:01:27.048
Like that $80 supermarket payment.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.470
A Record is your explanation of what happened.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.380
Groceries for $50 is a Record.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.963
The gift for $30 is another.

00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:42.323
An Allocation connects a Statement to a Record, and says how much of the Statement belongs there.

00:01:42.823 --> 00:01:49.746
Here, $50 of the supermarket payment is allocated to Groceries, and the other $30 to the gift.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.920
That’s the first pattern:

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.580
one Statement, split into several Records.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.305
It’s how you untangle a mixed shopping trip.

00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:00.107
The second pattern goes the other way:

00:02:00.427 --> 00:02:03.287
several Statements, combined into one Record.

00:02:03.707 --> 00:02:11.577
Your $90 dinner payment and your friend’s $60 transfer both belong to one Dinner Record, which comes to your real share:

00:02:11.877 --> 00:02:12.948
$30.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.340
And most of the time, it’s simply one to one.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.274
A $12 lunch is one Statement, explained by one Lunch Record.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.419
Trying Finpoint first

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:28.299
On your first visit, the Dashboard offers demo data:

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:33.732
three accounts and five months of Records, so you can see what a lived-in Finpoint looks like.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.650
Help also has practice lessons.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:42.998
They use their own sample Statements, so you can try a split or a Pending Record without touching your data.

00:02:43.867 --> 00:02:46.357
When you’re ready, start with your own bank activity.

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:47.518
That’s next.

00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:50.846
Importing your bank statements

00:02:51.446 --> 00:02:53.916
Finpoint starts from your bank’s own records.

00:02:54.176 --> 00:02:57.395
You download them from your bank, then bring them into Finpoint.

00:02:58.267 --> 00:03:03.887
Finpoint can read exports from DBS, OCBC, UOB and Revolut.

00:03:04.297 --> 00:03:08.618
Depending on the bank, that’s a CSV file or an Excel spreadsheet.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:14.010
Each Statement belongs to an Account, which simply mirrors one of your bank accounts.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:25.758
Import your OCBC 360 Account and your UOB savings account, and Finpoint keeps their Statements apart, using the account details inside each file.

00:03:26.633 --> 00:03:30.473
Some exports, like Revolut’s, don’t say which account they came from.

00:03:30.813 --> 00:03:34.348
For those, Finpoint asks you to pick or name the account.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.556
Download a CSV or Excel export from your bank, once for each account you want in Finpoint.

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:45.267
Open Importer, choose your bank, and add your files.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.241
Choose Import, and check how many new Statements came in.

00:03:49.967 --> 00:03:54.487
Your new Statements are now waiting in Workflows, under Allocate to Records.

00:03:54.777 --> 00:03:56.177
That’s where we’ll go next.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:58.792
Workflows

00:03:59.392 --> 00:04:01.832
Workflows is where you’ll spend most of your time.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:09.187
Its first tab, Allocate to Records, lists every Statement that still has money left to explain, newest first.

00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:14.547
When only part of a Statement has been explained, it shows how much is left.

00:04:14.907 --> 00:04:18.344
Once a Statement is fully explained, it leaves the list.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.820
The other two tabs tidy up your imports.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:29.210
Fix Duplicates catches activity your bank exported twice, and Replace Pending swaps a placeholder for the real thing.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:30.530
We’ll get to both.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.600
Two other pages help you look things up.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:37.510
Statements lists all your bank activity, explained or not.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.780
Records lists your explanations.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.595
Workflows only shows what still needs you.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.964
Explaining your first purchase

00:04:46.564 --> 00:04:48.774
Let’s explain a $12 lunch.

00:04:49.024 --> 00:04:52.484
In Allocate to Records, click the lunch Statement to select it.

00:04:52.804 --> 00:04:55.180
A bar appears with what you can do next.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.290
Choose Create Record.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.905
A new Record opens with the Statement already allocated, and its amount and date filled in.

00:05:03.633 --> 00:05:07.289
Give it a title, like Lunch, and pick a Category, like Dining Out.

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:11.927
Under Analytics you’ll also see Treatment and Monthly Budget.

00:05:12.317 --> 00:05:13.957
Leave those alone for now:

00:05:14.157 --> 00:05:17.538
your Category fills them in, and we’ll come back to them later.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.230
Choose Create Record to save.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:24.074
The lunch Statement leaves the list, because it’s fully explained.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.104
Splitting one payment

00:05:27.704 --> 00:05:30.064
Back to the $80 supermarket trip.

00:05:30.304 --> 00:05:32.599
Select it, and choose Create Record.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:39.560
Call it Groceries, and change both the Record’s amount and the Statement’s Allocation to $50.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:43.059
The other $30 stays unexplained for now.

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:48.526
Save, and the supermarket Statement stays in the list, with $30 left.

00:05:49.233 --> 00:05:54.953
Select it again, and create a second Record, Birthday gift, for the $30 left to allocate.

00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:57.536
Finpoint fills that amount in for you.

00:05:58.267 --> 00:06:03.361
Save, and the supermarket Statement leaves the list, because it’s now fully explained.

00:06:04.233 --> 00:06:07.213
Now one Statement is explained by two Records:

00:06:07.493 --> 00:06:11.025
$50 of groceries, and a $30 gift.

00:06:12.633 --> 00:06:14.938
Combining payments into one Record

00:06:15.538 --> 00:06:16.978
Now the shared dinner.

00:06:17.278 --> 00:06:18.808
Select both Statements:

00:06:18.928 --> 00:06:22.828
the $90 you paid, and the $60 your friend sent back.

00:06:23.188 --> 00:06:24.788
The bar shows their total:

00:06:24.838 --> 00:06:25.889
$30.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.590
Choose Create Record, and call it Dinner with Sam.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:34.950
Both Statements are allocated, and the Record comes to $30, your real share.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.310
Save, and two Statements are explained by a single Record.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:44.247
Your spending shows the $30 you really spent, not $90.

00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:47.249
Where your numbers come from

00:06:47.849 --> 00:06:51.339
With a few Records saved, your Dashboard starts to fill in.

00:06:51.759 --> 00:06:56.523
Every total on it is calculated from Records, never directly from Statements.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.440
That’s why importing alone doesn’t change your spending.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:03.574
A Statement only counts once you’ve explained it.

00:07:04.433 --> 00:07:09.093
And because it uses your explanations, the Dashboard shows what you really spent:

00:07:09.493 --> 00:07:14.220
$30 on dinner, not $90, and groceries apart from the gift.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:21.745
Categories this month breaks your spending down by Category, so the Categories you choose shape everything you see here.

00:07:23.367 --> 00:07:24.615
Pending Records

00:07:25.215 --> 00:07:28.425
Sometimes you know about spending before your bank shows all of it.

00:07:28.735 --> 00:07:30.787
Finpoint lets you record it anyway.

00:07:31.667 --> 00:07:38.667
Say you paid $200 for two concert tickets, and your friend Jo will pay you back $100 next week.

00:07:39.077 --> 00:07:44.469
You can already create the Record for your $100 share, from the $200 payment.

00:07:45.333 --> 00:07:49.493
The amounts don’t match yet, $100 against $200.

00:07:49.833 --> 00:07:53.172
Finpoint warns you, and saves the Record as Pending.

00:07:54.033 --> 00:07:58.274
Pending Records are marked in your Records list, so nothing gets forgotten.

00:07:59.133 --> 00:08:04.253
When Jo’s $100 arrives, select it and choose Allocate to record.

00:08:04.733 --> 00:08:08.247
Finpoint suggests the Pending Record it matches exactly.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.370
The Record now combines both payments.

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:15.251
Save, and because the amounts tally, it’s no longer Pending.

00:08:16.133 --> 00:08:18.883
So a Record is Pending for one of two reasons.

00:08:19.153 --> 00:08:21.953
Its amounts don’t tally yet, like the concert.

00:08:22.263 --> 00:08:25.023
Or one of its Statements is still Pending at the bank.

00:08:25.393 --> 00:08:29.282
Either way, Pending means the Record isn’t fully confirmed yet.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.140
Pending Statements

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:36.440
A Pending Statement is bank activity that isn’t confirmed yet.

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:41.080
Some imports, like Revolut’s, bring card payments in as Pending.

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:45.648
And if your bank takes days to show a payment, you can add one yourself.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.720
On the Statements page, choose Create Pending Statement.

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:53.709
Pick the account, then enter the amount and a short description.

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:57.043
It appears in Workflows, marked Pending.

00:08:57.393 --> 00:08:59.453
Explain it like any other Statement.

00:08:59.803 --> 00:09:03.935
The Record counts straight away, but stays Pending while its Statement does.

00:09:04.667 --> 00:09:08.557
When the confirmed Statement is imported, open Replace Pending.

00:09:09.017 --> 00:09:14.148
Pick the Pending Statement, and Finpoint suggests the imported Statements it could become.

00:09:14.867 --> 00:09:18.027
Review the swap, and choose Replace pending Statement.

00:09:18.447 --> 00:09:24.836
The Allocations move to the confirmed Statement, the Pending one disappears, and nothing is counted twice.

00:09:26.467 --> 00:09:28.236
Fixing duplicate Statements

00:09:28.836 --> 00:09:32.606
Sometimes a bank changes a payment’s description between exports.

00:09:32.936 --> 00:09:36.779
Import both files, and the same payment arrives twice.

00:09:37.633 --> 00:09:44.143
Finpoint spots Statements on the same account, day and amount, and marks them as possible duplicates.

00:09:44.553 --> 00:09:47.637
Open Fix Duplicates in Workflows to review them.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.940
Choose the Statement to keep, then Keep 1, delete 1.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:55.984
If they really are two payments, choose Not duplicates instead.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:59.920
Finpoint shows what will happen before anything is deleted.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:04.722
Allocations follow the Statement you keep, so your Records stay explained.

00:10:06.333 --> 00:10:08.055
Finding and fixing Records

00:10:08.655 --> 00:10:10.905
To find something later, open Records.

00:10:11.195 --> 00:10:15.075
Search by title, or filter by date, Category or amount.

00:10:15.933 --> 00:10:22.924
Open a Record to see its Statements, then choose Edit Record to change any field, or the Allocations behind it.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:29.308
Statements works the same way for your bank activity, and each Account has a page of its own.

00:10:30.167 --> 00:10:36.747
If you always look at the same period, set default filters in Settings, and every list opens on that range.

00:10:38.367 --> 00:10:39.298
Categories

00:10:39.898 --> 00:10:43.228
Categories are the most important choice you make on a Record.

00:10:43.628 --> 00:10:47.635
They say what it was for, and the Dashboard breaks your spending down by them.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.090
Lunch and dinner with friends are Dining Out.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:53.650
The supermarket run is Groceries.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:57.010
The birthday gift goes under Gifts & Donations.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:03.853
Finpoint starts you with a sensible set, and you can rename them, add your own, or nest one under another.

00:11:04.733 --> 00:11:07.433
Each Category also carries two defaults:

00:11:07.573 --> 00:11:09.683
a treatment, and a Monthly Budget.

00:11:09.983 --> 00:11:12.523
That’s why you could ignore those fields earlier.

00:11:12.803 --> 00:11:14.405
The Category fills them in.

00:11:16.033 --> 00:11:17.188
Statement rules

00:11:17.788 --> 00:11:20.998
Some payments come back every month, like your phone bill.

00:11:21.418 --> 00:11:25.366
A Statement rule recognises them, and fills in the Record for you.

00:11:26.233 --> 00:11:28.313
Open Rules and choose New rule.

00:11:28.613 --> 00:11:31.793
Give it a name, and the text to look for in the description.

00:11:32.173 --> 00:11:34.647
Finpoint shows the Statements it matches.

00:11:35.367 --> 00:11:36.897
Then choose what it fills in:

00:11:37.057 --> 00:11:40.607
a title, a Category, even People or a Location.

00:11:40.977 --> 00:11:42.997
Autofill fills a field in for you.

00:11:43.187 --> 00:11:45.803
Suggest offers it as a one-click choice.

00:11:46.667 --> 00:11:50.947
Next time you create a Record from a matching Statement, the fields are ready.

00:11:51.247 --> 00:11:52.981
You can still change any of them.

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:55.482
Treatments

00:11:56.082 --> 00:11:58.712
Treatment decides how the Dashboard counts a Record:

00:11:59.082 --> 00:12:02.909
as income, as spending, as saving, or not at all.

00:12:03.767 --> 00:12:05.347
Your salary is Income.

00:12:05.687 --> 00:12:11.107
Lunch and groceries are Spending, and so is a refund, which lowers what you spent.

00:12:11.527 --> 00:12:16.317
Putting $500 into an investment account is Saving / investment.

00:12:16.597 --> 00:12:21.540
And moving money between two accounts you track is a Transfer, which isn’t counted at all.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.460
On the Dashboard, Income and Spending make up your totals and charts.

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:31.340
Saving and investment is kept out of them, in its own section further down.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:33.558
And Transfers don’t appear at all.

00:12:34.433 --> 00:12:36.613
Your Category sets the treatment for you.

00:12:36.923 --> 00:12:40.830
Change it on a single Record only when that Record is an exception.

00:12:42.433 --> 00:12:43.769
Monthly Budgets

00:12:44.369 --> 00:12:47.639
A Monthly Budget groups your spending for planning month by month.

00:12:47.999 --> 00:12:49.699
Finpoint starts you with four:

00:12:49.889 --> 00:12:52.837
Daily, Recurring, Irregular and Travel.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:55.530
Daily is the one to watch.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:13:02.480
It holds everyday spending, like food and transport, and the Dashboard forecasts where it’s heading by the end of the month.

00:13:03.333 --> 00:13:10.263
To give Daily an amount, choose Edit beside it on the Dashboard, and set a Monthly amount, say $500.

00:13:10.967 --> 00:13:13.417
The Amount rule says how far it reaches.

00:13:13.757 --> 00:13:16.517
Override one month changes this month only.

00:13:16.807 --> 00:13:19.670
Override default applies from this month onward.

00:13:20.533 --> 00:13:24.003
The Dashboard then shows how the month is going against the plan.

00:13:24.323 --> 00:13:27.203
A Monthly Budget doesn’t move or hold any money.

00:13:27.553 --> 00:13:29.127
It’s simply a comparison.

00:13:30.733 --> 00:13:32.070
Monthly Records

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.530
Next to the Dashboard’s Overview is Monthly Records:

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:39.704
every Record in the month, grouped by day, with each day’s total.

00:13:40.567 --> 00:13:46.218
Click any part of a chart on the Overview, and Monthly Records opens on just those Records.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:51.350
At the bottom of the Overview, Review this month links to anything that needs you:

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:56.264
Pending Records, Pending Statements, and spending without a Monthly Budget.

00:13:57.867 --> 00:13:59.182
Reading the Overview

00:13:59.782 --> 00:14:02.652
The top of the Overview answers the big questions:

00:14:02.912 --> 00:14:05.832
what came in, what you spent, and what’s left.

00:14:06.272 --> 00:14:10.603
Each number is compared with the last two months, at the same point in the month.

00:14:11.467 --> 00:14:15.707
Choose a Monthly Budget at the top, and the spending charts narrow to it.

00:14:16.027 --> 00:14:18.610
Cards marked Total always show everything.

00:14:19.467 --> 00:14:26.860
The spending chart shows this month so far, where it’s heading, and the months before, so you can see early if you’re running ahead.

00:14:27.733 --> 00:14:34.921
Further down, Categories this month and their trend show what changed, and Biggest changes calls out the largest moves.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:38.700
The calendar colours each day by how much you spent.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:40.705
Pick a day to see its Records.

00:14:42.333 --> 00:14:44.095
Budgets for trips and events

00:14:44.695 --> 00:14:50.000
A Budget is for spending with its own start and end, like a week in Bali, or a wedding.

00:14:50.867 --> 00:14:53.755
Create one with a name, an amount and its dates.

00:14:54.467 --> 00:15:00.587
Automatic attach adds every Record dated inside the period, including income and transfers.

00:15:01.007 --> 00:15:05.741
To choose the Records yourself, turn it off, and attach them from the Budget’s page.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:09.760
A Budget is its own small dashboard for that period:

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.640
what you’ve spent, what’s left, and your pace.

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:16.195
It’s separate from your monthly Dashboard, and doesn’t change it.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:19.394
Backups and Google Drive

00:15:19.994 --> 00:15:23.414
Finpoint keeps your data in this browser, on this device.

00:15:23.814 --> 00:15:29.702
There’s no Finpoint database, and your finances never pass through our servers, so we never see them.

00:15:30.567 --> 00:15:34.187
That means keeping a copy is up to you, and there are two ways.

00:15:34.587 --> 00:15:36.317
The first is a backup file.

00:15:36.537 --> 00:15:42.596
Download it from the Data page whenever you like, and restore it later, on this device or a new one.

00:15:43.467 --> 00:15:45.317
The second is Google Drive.

00:15:45.627 --> 00:15:53.399
Connect it once, and Finpoint keeps a copy in a hidden folder in your own Drive, automatically, so your devices stay in step.

00:15:54.267 --> 00:15:59.187
If the same data changed on two devices, Finpoint asks which copy to keep.

00:15:59.547 --> 00:16:02.389
It keeps one whole copy, never a mix of both.

00:16:03.267 --> 00:16:06.645
Either way, your data only goes to places you control.

00:16:08.267 --> 00:16:09.289
Your routine

00:16:09.889 --> 00:16:11.609
Here’s a routine that works.

00:16:11.769 --> 00:16:13.539
Import your latest statements.

00:16:13.809 --> 00:16:17.029
Fix any duplicates, and explain what’s new in Workflows.

00:16:17.299 --> 00:16:18.659
Check anything Pending.

00:16:18.889 --> 00:16:20.319
Glance at your Dashboard.

00:16:20.399 --> 00:16:21.534
And keep a backup.

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:25.780
With Google Drive connected, that happens automatically.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.281
If not, download a backup file every so often.

00:16:30.133 --> 00:16:33.493
And whenever you’re unsure, choose Help at the top of any page.

00:16:33.863 --> 00:16:36.826
Every chapter of this guide is there, with pictures.
